As far as I understand, the reason this hasn't been done so far is that
bones files *aren't* standardized -- different CPUs will produce
incompatible bones, although some people have talked about using
a standard "external representation of data" format to produce bones
files.

So I have some questions.  Is there a utility to *convert* system-specific
bones files into files with a standardized data representation?  If so,
this could be used for the exchange.  If not, is it worth somebody's
while, as this person proposed, to maintain an archive site with
separate directories for each platform -- are there compiled-in
things like pathnames or such in bones files that would make even
same-platform bones useless across different specific machines,
and would the effort of maintaining this archive be worth the
results?  Would this whole idea really be wise, anyway?  People
could, and probably would, sometimes create impossibly hard
levels in debug mode and force-save them as bones.  Individual
game maintainers would usually want to review bones files first,
by backing them up and running through them in debug mode.  A
lot of work, eh?  But it could be fun, anyway.


